course: dictionary
lesson: 3909
title: Frilled
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Frilled comes from frill. A frill is a strip of cloth with folds or waves sewn on the edge, like the trim on a dress or a cuff. So frilled means having a frill, or having a decorative wavy edge.
explain: Look at the words around it. «The frilled lizard spreads a frill around its neck, held open by rods of cartilage, the bendy stuff in your ears, like an umbrella, to look bigger and frightening.» The clues are spreads, umbrella and bigger, so frill must be a big wavy flap of skin around the neck.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it in. A «frilled collar» is a collar with a frill, and a frilled neck is a neck with a wavy flap of skin. Both still make sense, so your meaning works.
fact: Frilled means having a frill.
fact: A frill is a strip of cloth with folds or waves sewn on the edge.
fact: The «frilled lizard» lives in northern Australia and has a ruffled flap of skin around its neck.
fact: The frilled lizard opens its frill to look bigger and scare enemies.
fact: In clothes, «frilled» means with a decorative wavy edge, as in a «frilled collar».
exercise: «The frilled lizard spreads a frill around its neck.» What is the frill? | *A ruffled flap of skin around the neck | A type of cloth | A hard shell on its back | A tail that curls up
exercise: «She wore a dress with a frilled collar.» What does frilled mean here? | Very dirty | Covered in paint | Torn in pieces | *With a decorative wavy edge
exercise: Where does the frilled lizard live? | In the Arctic snow | *In northern Australia | In the Amazon rainforest | On the moon
test: Why does the frilled lizard open its frill? | *To look bigger and frightening to enemies | To keep warm in the snow | To sing to other lizards | To catch fish in a river
test: What holds the frill open? | *Rods of cartilage, the bendy stuff in your ears | Strings of wool | Bones of the tail | Rods of metal
exam: Which word fits best? «Grandma sewed a ____ edge on the pillow.» | silent | flat | *frilled | sharp
